# Source: "Hoare Logic 2.docx", exercise 1 b), solved in "Hoare Logic 2 Solved.docx".
id: square-36
title: A weakest precondition with two solutions
topic: Sequential composition
level: 2
source: Hoare Logic 2, exercise 1 b
tags: assignment, sequence, disjunction, consequence

task:
  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition
  $Q$, statement by statement from the last one. Then use the consequence
  rule to decide whether the triple is valid.

pre: x = 5
post: x = 36
range: 8

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  y := x + 1;      // i1
  {R1}
  x := y * y       // i2
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of x := y * y
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The precondition of an assignment is obtained by substituting the
  assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment expression ($y * y$) in the
  postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(x := y * y, Q)
  = (x = 36)[x <- y * y]
  = y * y = 36             // the x of Q is replaced by y * y
set: R1 = y * y = 36

step: R2, the weakest precondition of y := x + 1
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment expression
  ($x + 1$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R1$. The
  expression goes in parentheses, because it is multiplied.
calc:
  wp(y := x + 1, R1)
  = (y * y = 36)[y <- x + 1]
  = (x + 1) * (x + 1) = 36       // both y of R1 are replaced by (x + 1)
  = x + 1 = 6 || x + 1 = -6      // the integers whose square is 36 are 6 and -6
  = x = 5 || x + 1 = -6          // subtract 1 from both sides of x + 1 = 6
  = x = 5 || x = -7              // subtract 1 from both sides of x + 1 = -6
set: R2 = x = 5 || x = -7

step: The precondition must imply the weakest precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  $R2$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R2$.
  >>> «x = 5 : P» ==> «x = 5 || x = -7 : R2»
vc: x = 5 ==> x = 5 || x = -7
why:
  This is the law $A ==> A || B$: when one side of a disjunction holds,
  the disjunction holds.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The weakest precondition has two solutions, $x = 5$ and $x = -7$,
  because squaring loses the sign. The precondition $P$ keeps only one of
  them. So $P$ is stronger than the weakest precondition, and the
  consequence rule applies.
